{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Good data. The QKV fusion helps for the smaller cases (where projection cost dominates) but not for the large L=256 case (where attention matmul dominates). Overall, fused_qkv looks better. Let me think about other optimizations.\n\nKey insight: for the largest case (B1L256C1280H20), the current approach is 0.412x ref — and the attention cost dominates. Let me profile where time is spent more carefully and think about algorithmic improvements.\n\nOne big idea: instead of computing Gram matrix G = x @ x^T (which is O(B L² C)) and then p @ G (O(B H L²)), note that we need `||p @ x||²` per row. But we already have v = x @ wv^T. Wait, no — we need `||u||` where u = p @ x (in C dims), not `||p @ v||`. \n\nActually wait — we compute `p_mix @ v` for the output. And we compute `||p_mix @ x||²` for the ratio. Two different things. The Gram trick saves us from computing p_mix @ x explicitly (which would be O(B H L C)).\n\nLet me think about what's the biggest cost for the large-L case:\n- logits = q @ k^T: O(B H L² D)\n- softmax: O(B H L²)\n- p1 logits computation: O(B H L²)\n- p @ G: 2x O(B H L²) (with broadcasting)\n- p_mix @ v: O(B H L² D)\n\nFor L=256, H=20, D=64, C=1280:\n- q@k^T: 20 * 256² * 64 = 84M ops\n- p@G: 20 * 256² = 1.3M ops (negligible!)\n- p_mix@v: 20 * 256² * 64 = 84M ops\n- projections: 3 * 256 * 1280 * 1280 = ~1.26B ops — wait, that's huge!\n\nWait, let me recalculate. H*D = 20*64 = 1280. So projection is (B*L) × C @ C × (H*D) = 256 × 1280 × 1280 = 419M per projection, ×3 = 1.26B. But q@k^T is B×H×L×D @ B×H×D×L = 1*20*256*64*256 = 83.9M. That's way smaller.\n\nHmm, but the fused QKV is 3 × the size... actually it's the same total FLOPs. The fusion just reduces overhead.\n\nWait, then why is the current approach 0.41x the reference? Because the reference uses einsum which is slow, plus it does p0 @ x and p1 @ x (each O(B H L² C) = 20*256²*1280 = 1.68B ops!) which is massive. We replaced that with the Gram trick + p@v (which is O(B H L² D) = 84M). That's the main win.\n\nSo the biggest remaining costs are:\n1. Q/K/V projections: ~1.26B FLOPs (but we can't reduce these — they're necessary)\n2. q@k^T: ~84M\n3. p_mix@v: ~84M\n4. Various overhead\n\nLet me think about what else we can save:\n\nActually wait, the v projection is x @ wv^T → v of shape (B, L, H, D). Then p_mix @ v gives output of shape (B, H, L, D). This is correct.\n\nHmm, one thought: for the alpha=1.0 case (case 6), we only need p0, so we skip p1 computation entirely. That's already done.\n\nAnother thought: can we use `torch.bmm` more efficiently than the current batched approach? The current permute/reshape already creates (B*H, L, D) tensors effectively. Let me try reshaping q,k,v to (B*H, L, D) and using bmm directly — this might be slightly more efficient than the 4D tensor approach.\n\nAlso, let me try a few more micro-optimizations:\n1. Reshape to (B*H, L, D) for bmm instead of (B, H, L, D)\n2. Fuse the inv_sqrt_d multiplication into the weights or logits\n3. Use `addmm_` or in-place ops where safe\n4. Try computing G as `x.matmul(x.transpose(-1,-2))` — already fast\n\nLet me benchmark the reshaping approach:"}}
